{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261027T225440Z-e72216f1", "ts": "2026-10-27T22:57:19Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.8.0-dev.2", "branch": "feature/1078-unloaded-tripwire"}, "body": {"base_commit": "eb4de60ea330e0d9cfc8e1a40665936e76b2d2ad", "base_tree": "d28058350bc526e7fcfae56e34065884436ebebd", "head_tree": "a235c57a3f2aa95b71adc8a0395a0d66e3e289e1", "head_commit": "cc480c377fb2f7228cff7747c4b6200212e4eaca", "dispatch_commit": "cc480c377fb2f7228cff7747c4b6200212e4eaca", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["documentation/project-structure.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/anchor_archive.py", "plugin/lib/anchor_repair.py", "plugin/lib/init_product.py", "plugin/lib/install_reference_probes.py", "plugin/lib/migrate_plugin.py", "plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py", "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/migrate/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md", "tests/test_anchor_repair.py", "tests/test_hook_argument_shape.py", "tests/test_plugin_migrate.py", "tests/test_unloaded_tripwire.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-unloaded-tripwire.md", ".prawduct/changes/unloaded-tripwire.md", "documentation/project-structure.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/anchor_archive.py", "plugin/lib/anchor_repair.py", "plugin/lib/init_product.py", "plugin/lib/install_reference_probes.py", "plugin/lib/migrate_plugin.py", "plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py", "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/migrate/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md", "tests/test_anchor_repair.py", "tests/test_hook_argument_shape.py", "tests/test_plugin_migrate.py", "tests/test_unloaded_tripwire.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-unloaded-tripwire.md", ".prawduct/changes/unloaded-tripwire.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The tripwire tells a git-worktree session governance is off even when the plugin loaded", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: the hook copies plugin_activation's exact-path match, but it now runs inside every session. plugin_activation itself (report_lines, the DOCTOR and MIGRATE branches) records that a repo whose project/local-scope record names the primary checkout still loads the plugin in a git worktree, a case that module only ever saw from outside. In a `claude --worktree` session of such a repo, CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR is the worktree, so the hook prints the loud 'PRAWDUCT GOVERNANCE IS NOT ACTIVE ... no session banner ... Tell the user before any work' line while the banner is right there. That breaks the plan's Success bullet 3 ('It never accuses falsely'), and the message excuses only --plugin-dir. On this machine discodon, hallucinote and samsung-frame-art-loader all carry project-scope records. Fix: in _VERDICT_PY, also match the main checkout. Read `<project>/.git`; when it is a file holding `gitdir: \u2026/.git/worktrees/<n>`, take the commondir's parent and compare it as well. That uses only stdlib calls available in 3.0. Or, at minimum, downgrade a worktree miss to the soft could-not-verify line. Add a fixture with a .git file pointing at a common dir whose parent is the recorded projectPath, and assert the hook is silent or soft there. test_verdict_agrees_with_plugin_activation cannot catch this, because both sides share the rule.", "files": ["plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py", "tests/test_unloaded_tripwire.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "ensure_tripwire raises AttributeError on settings with \"hooks\": null", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: `_session_start` reads data.get('hooks') is None as 'no hooks' and returns [], so tripwire_state says ABSENT. ensure_tripwire then calls data.setdefault('hooks', {}), which returns the existing None, and hooks.get('SessionStart', []) raises. The crash reaches transform_settings (init-product, /prawduct:migrate) and `install-tripwire --apply`, which prints a traceback instead of the documented exit 1. `\"hooks\": {\"SessionStart\": null}` is already treated as MALFORMED, so the two null shapes disagree. Fix: in _session_start, separate a missing key from an explicit null with `'hooks' not in data`, so null reads as MALFORMED. Or have ensure_tripwire replace a None value. Pin it with one fixture.", "files": ["plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "note", "title": "init-product and migrate leave out the tripwire without saying so when hooks is malformed", "recommendation": "Instance. When an existing settings file has a MALFORMED hooks shape (for example SessionStart is an object), transform_settings calls ensure_tripwire. That call returns False and nothing is reported, so the repo onboards or migrates without the hook. On every run init_product's _install_reference_present then returns False, so the file is listed as 'edited' while the tripwire is never written. The tripwire-missing probe skips MALFORMED by design, so only a later doctor run shows it. Consider adding a warning to init-product's and migrate's result when tripwire_state is MALFORMED after transform, so the owner learns at onboarding time.", "files": ["plugin/lib/migrate_plugin.py", "plugin/lib/init_product.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "Committed-footprint claims still say a governed repo commits only an install reference, and governance code stays in the plugin", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: the change put executable governance code (a POSIX sh + python3 check) into every product's committed .claude/settings.json, and updated only the one project-structure.md tree line, so the sentences that describe the committed footprint, or that rest on 'nothing governance-bearing lives in the repo', no longer match. Sites found: .prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md Design Intent #5 ('The plugin writes nothing into a repo it governs, except its own version marker and the guidance it injects ... Governance code and methodology ship in the plugin and stay there') and its Distribution paragraph (~line 230, 'A governed repo commits only an install reference'); documentation/project-structure.md:96 (the tree line was edited, the prose above it was not); README.md:122 and :224; plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md:9 and plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md:9 ('a product commits only the install reference plus its own .prawduct/ state'); plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md:52 (Check #4: 'the anchor is the only governance text a clone without the plugin loads', which the tripwire's own output now contradicts). Who acts wrongly: an owner or maintainer reading architecture.md as the plan's governed_by artifact concludes no governance code is committed in product repos, then meets a 30-line shell script in their settings.json diff that no description admits to. A Direction clause in the same artifact gives a reason ('framework code stays in the plugin, read-only from the repo's perspective') that the tripwire's inlined verdict now tests. Fix the descriptions (architecture.md Design Intent #5 and Distribution, project-structure.md:96, README, the onboard and doctor intros, doctor Check #4). Do not edit the Direction clause; if it needs a reading, record a [DECISION] beneath it the way the 2026-10-14 and 2026-10-25 entries do. Search 'commits only', 'install reference', 'no framework files' and 'only governance text' across README.md, documentation/, plugin/ and .prawduct/artifacts/. The list above is what I found, not proven complete.", "files": ["documentation/project-structure.md", "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "TRIPWIRE_MATCHER copies the banner hook's matcher and nothing pins the two together", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. unloaded_tripwire.TRIPWIRE_MATCHER is a hand copy of the matcher on plugin/hooks/hooks.json's banner.py group. Its comment ('Same events the plugin's banner hook fires on, so \"no banner appeared\" and \"the tripwire would have spoken\" cover the same session starts'), the plan's mid-build decision and the anchor's new 'loaded if its session banner appeared' line all rely on the two being equal. tests/test_unloaded_tripwire.py never reads hooks.json. Add one assertion that TRIPWIRE_MATCHER equals the matcher of the hooks.json SessionStart group whose command runs banner.py. Per core.md, 'A rule you must RECALL at the right moment is its weakest form': this is a queryable claim. The cost is low because both fire at startup, so this is a NOTE.", "files": ["plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py", "tests/test_unloaded_tripwire.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "TRIPWIRE_TAG's version number is inert, so 'Bump the version when the command changes' instructs a step nothing reads", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. _is_tripwire matches on TRIPWIRE_TAG.rsplit(' ', 1)[0], so it drops the 'v1'. tripwire_state grades PRESENT only when the group equals TRIPWIRE_GROUP exactly, so any change to the command is already graded STALE without a bump. The comment says ':func:`tripwire_state` reports an older copy stale' because of the bump, which it does not depend on. A maintainer either follows a no-op instruction or skips it with no effect. Either delete the version and the bump sentence and keep the tag as a bare identity marker, or make the comment relational (the version is informational and staleness is whole-group equality). No behaviour change is wanted.", "files": ["plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "plugin_activation's module docstring still says the not-loaded detector can only run from outside the repo", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. plugin/lib/plugin_activation.py's docstring argues 'the detector has to run from *outside* the repo \u2014 from the onboarding session ... why its caller is /prawduct:onboard rather than a probe registry'. This change ships a second, in-repo detector, a project hook that mirrors this module's rule, and the tripwire's own test pins parity with it. The 'cannot be a session probe' half is still true. The 'has to run from outside the repo' half is now false and leaves out the inlined copy that has to stay in step with this module. Make the sentence relational: plugin-delivered probes cannot see this state, and a project-level hook (lib/unloaded_tripwire.py) mirrors this rule in-repo. This is prose wording, so it is a NOTE.", "files": ["plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "The plugin id is defined three times, and the tripwire takes the copy that is not the install contract's", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Before this diff, PLUGIN_ID was already defined in three places: plugin_activation.py:54 (literal), repo_toggle.py:33 (literal) and migrate_plugin.py:99 (derived from INSTALL_REFERENCE['enabledPlugins']). unloaded_tripwire imports plugin_activation's literal, and install_reference_probes imports migrate_plugin's derived one. So the tripwire's install command and the reference it is written beside come from different sources, and they agree only because the literals happen to match. Make the two literals import migrate_plugin.PLUGIN_ID (or one shared constant) so that INSTALL_REFERENCE is the single source. A rename is unlikely, so this is a NOTE.", "files": ["plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py", "plugin/lib/install_reference_probes.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "Plan goals still marked inferred: #1078 item-3 scope and the --plugin-dir false warning", "recommendation": "Instance. Goal 5 says a goal still marked inferred or hedged is a WARNING. The plan's Goals section says 'the owner's choice among the parts is inferred' and marks two choices *Inferred.* First, item 3 of #1078 (a doctor report) is in scope only as far as doctor installing the hook. Second, a session started with --plugin-dir and no install record gets the loud 'NOT ACTIVE' line. That second one hits framework developers in every governed repo they open that way. What it costs: the owner merges a scope choice he never confirmed, and either expected the item-3 report or did not want the --plugin-dir noise. Fix: get the owner's one-line confirm or correction for both choices, and record it in the plan's Goals or mid-build decisions, not only in conversation.", "files": ["plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py", "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions for the owner (three not settled by the plan)", "recommendation": "Scope: none. This is the owner digest Goal 5 requires. What settled each decision: (1) The Step-0 harness behaviour was checked against code.claude.com hooks and permissions docs on 2026-10-27. (2) A POSIX sh + python3-3.0 verdict mirrors plugin_activation and is pinned by test_verdict_agrees_with_plugin_activation. (3) A missing manifest gets the soft 'unknown' line, following the module's rule. (4) The matcher is the banner's own; timeout 10s. (5) The hook is kept out of INSTALL_REFERENCE, because that contract is compared leaf by leaf. (6) The writers are transform_settings plus the new install-tripwire subcommand. Not settled by the plan, so these need the owner: (a) a new `prawduct-hook install-tripwire` subcommand instead of doctor hand-editing JSON; (b) a new info advisory `install-reference`/`tripwire-missing`; (c) the new anchor_repair.SUBSTANCE row (`session banner`), which re-grades every owner-edited anchor across the fleet from ok to `stale-modified` (doctor degraded, 'yours to reword'). Consumer impact was investigated: no other settings reader is affected, since legacy_hook_events/_is_prawduct_hook match only `product-hook` and repo_toggle writes only enabledPlugins. No alignment-pass miss was observed.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/install_reference_probes.py", "plugin/lib/anchor_repair.py"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "Tripwire reports every verdict-script failure as 'python3 could not be run'", "recommendation": "Instance. In TRIPWIRE_COMMAND, `v=$(python3 -c '...' ... 2>/dev/null) || v=\"python3 could not be run\"` throws away stderr and maps any non-zero exit to the same reason word. So an uncaught exception in the verdict (a Claude Code manifest shape the script did not anticipate) reads as a missing interpreter. The user-facing outcome is still correct, because the soft could-not-verify line never accuses. Only the diagnosis misleads, so no edit is required. If wanted: keep `python3 could not be run` for exit 127 / command-not-found, and give other non-zero exits a distinct word such as `verdict failed`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py"], "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "Migrate/init silently skip the tripwire when settings `hooks` is malformed, and the probe stays quiet", "recommendation": "Instance. transform_settings calls unloaded_tripwire.ensure_tripwire(data) and ignores the result. On a MALFORMED `hooks` / `hooks.SessionStart` shape it writes nothing and reports success. probe_tripwire_missing also deliberately does not fire on MALFORMED. Only a manual /prawduct:doctor run (install-tripwire exit 1) surfaces it. The case is rare, and Claude Code may reject such settings anyway, so this is a NOTE. If it matters, have the init/migrate result name a skipped tripwire, the way install-tripwire's dry-run does.", "files": ["plugin/lib/migrate_plugin.py", "plugin/lib/install_reference_probes.py"], "fid": "R-12"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog item appears resolved: brookstalley/prawduct#1078", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Backlog item appears resolved: brookstalley/prawduct#1078 'governance: an unloaded plugin fails open with nothing saying so' (open in cache, and the change fragment's `closes:` names it). Verify it, then call `/prawduct:backlog update brookstalley/prawduct#1078 status=shipped closed-by=scope:unloaded-tripwire` when the backlog skill's 'When to mark shipped' rule says. Other cited ids resolve: #1097 is already shipped, and #928 is open and correctly out of scope.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/unloaded-tripwire.md"], "fid": "R-13"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "C-B3: open anchor-probe item in a touched area \u2014 brookstalley/prawduct#797", "recommendation": "Scope: none. The diff extends anchor_repair.SUBSTANCE and the archived-anchor roster. Open item brookstalley/prawduct#797 ('probes: nothing surfaces a stale or over-promising PRAWDUCT:ANCHOR') lives in that area, and no chunk updated it. Assess whether this work changes its status or text (it likely does not resolve it: reanchor is doctor-run, not a probe).", "files": ["plugin/lib/anchor_repair.py"], "fid": "R-14"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings Cross-Check: no regression against the loaded corpus", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Read core.md, authoring.md, gates.md, hook-surface.md, release.md and tests.md (the --for-diff list). Rules that bore on this change and were honoured: 'An advisory's printed recommendation ships to every consumer repo \u2014 test its EFFECT' (test_probe_is_inert_once_the_doctor_repair_ran); 'A channel that is produced and never consumed is a DEFECT' (the probe routes to doctor, and doctor runs install-tripwire); 'A new op whose NAME EXTENDS an existing one silently widens every prefix-matching Bash grant' (no op shares the install-tripwire prefix); 'A docstring or comment is a claim' (unloaded_tripwire's one-owner/one-reader docstring matches its callers); 'Prose that REPLACES a deleted control is load-bearing logic: pin every rendered branch' (inactive, unknown, missing, and no-python branches are each tested). No learnings rules were added this cycle, and no implicit supersession was found.", "fid": "R-15"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "Records Pass: build plan and change record clear no bar", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Covered files_oracle: .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-unloaded-tripwire.md and .prawduct/changes/unloaded-tripwire.md. The change record's claims match the code: INSTALL_REFERENCE and contract-drift are unchanged; the writers go through ensure_tripwire; the soft line never says 'not installed'; ANCHOR_V6 is archived; and the Windows-without-Git-Bash gap is disclosed in the consumer note. One imprecision clears no bar: 'replace a stale copy in place' (ensure_tripwire removes the old copy and appends the new group at the end of SessionStart). No edit is wanted.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-unloaded-tripwire.md", ".prawduct/changes/unloaded-tripwire.md"], "fid": "R-16"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 3, "note": 13}, "observations": [], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": "unloaded-tripwire", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01", "02", "03"], "chunks_proposed": ["01", "02", "03"], "chunk": "01", "base_reviewed": "eb4de60ea330e0d9cfc8e1a40665936e76b2d2ad", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-unloaded-tripwire.md", ".prawduct/changes/unloaded-tripwire.md", "documentation/project-structure.md", "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/migrate/SKILL.md", "plugin/ski